WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.640
If we discover aliens, we’ll probably&nbsp;
see them because of their farts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:13.920
The first signature of extraterrestrial life is&nbsp;
likely to be unnatural compositions of gasses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.920
in the atmosphere of a distant exoplanet. Think&nbsp;
of the Earth, the atmosphere is full of oxygen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.960
and this is only true because it keeps on&nbsp;
being replenished by plant and microbial life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:31.280
If life disappeared so would the oxygen. So if we&nbsp;
see a distant planet with an atmosphere where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.360
proportions of gasses are inexplicable by the laws&nbsp;
of chemistry alone, like lots of oxygen, or a mix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:43.840
of carbon dioxide and methane but with little&nbsp;
carbon monoxide, this would be a biosignature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:50.160
a strong indicator that at least microbial life&nbsp;
exists there. To confirm any large creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.320
we’ll just have to visit. But how do we know what&nbsp;
chemicals the atmosphere of a distant planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:03.600
contains? The answer is atomic spectroscopy. If&nbsp;
we see a planet passing in front of it’s star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.040
some of that starlight is absorbed in a very&nbsp;
specific pattern called an atomic absorption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:15.520
spectrum. Each element has a specific pattern&nbsp;
like a barcode, so through careful analysis of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.160
the light it can tell us which gasses are&nbsp;
in the atmosphere and their proportions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.160
We already use this technique for other&nbsp;
space objects like stars and nebulae,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:31.120
measuring properties like temperature, density,&nbsp;
ionization and relative velocity. Think about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.760
cool this is! We can look into deep space and&nbsp;
get so much information, just from the light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.040
It is like a gift that the laws of physics have&nbsp;
given us and we’ve used it, most of our knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.720
of the universe comes from this technique.
I’ve previously mentioned atomic spectra in my map&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.840
of quantum physics video and because this subject&nbsp;
is so cool I made this video to dig deeper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.840
There are two kinds of atomic spectra: absorption&nbsp;
spectra which is where the light source is behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.280
a gas, and the gas absorbs and scatters certain&nbsp;
wavelengths of light leaving dark lines in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:11.200
spectrum of the star. The opposite is emission&nbsp;
spectra, which is the same process but seen from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.160
a different angle. Now you are only looking&nbsp;
at the re-radiated light of this nebula.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.720
Here it is worth noting a few things. A hot gas&nbsp;
on its own emits light as an emission spectrum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:28.240
here I’m drawing the intensity of the light on&nbsp;
the vertical axis. A hot solid emits a spectrum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.520
at all wavelengths called a thermal spectrum, and&nbsp;
so you can’t tell what the object is made of from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:39.280
only how hot it is. And a star emits a combination&nbsp;
of a thermal spectrum with an absorption spectrum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:45.680
superimposed on top which comes from their upper&nbsp;
layers. So in atomic spectra, why do we get these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.760
very specific lines? To answer that we need&nbsp;
to delve into the world of quantum mechanics.
&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.640
Light is absorbed and emitted by the electrons&nbsp;
in atoms and these electrons can only exist in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:04.080
certain energy states which, plotted for&nbsp;
hydrogen, look like this. The reason for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.280
these discrete energy states is that in quantum&nbsp;
mechanics electrons are described by a wave called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.440
a wavefunction, and this wave can only vibrate at&nbsp;
certain frequencies in the constraint of the atom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.160
Just like a guitar string only vibrates&nbsp;
at certain frequencies in the constraint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.840
of being tied to the guitar. These waves are&nbsp;
standing waves, and each higher energy wave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:31.680
includes an extra half-wavelength and oscillates&nbsp;
at a higher frequency. I’ve simplified the drawings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.800
to one dimension but in reality these are actually&nbsp;
three dimensional waves because the electron cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.320
is three dimensional. There are an infinite number&nbsp;
of energy levels because you can keep on adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.360
one more half-wavelength to the electron wave, but&nbsp;
the difference in energies between these levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.840
gets smaller and smaller because the difference&nbsp;
between the waves gets smaller and smaller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.800
The biggest difference in energy is between&nbsp;
the ground state and the first excited state. 
&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.240
When light shines on atoms, if it contains light&nbsp;
with the same energy as one of the distances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.040
between energy levels of the electron it can&nbsp;
absorb that light and the electron jumps to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.320
a higher energy. Then later it will jump back&nbsp;
down to the lowest energy, emitting the light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:20.080
in a random direction. This is why we see the&nbsp;
dark bands in the absorption spectrum, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.880
the electrons are absorbing the light then&nbsp;
scattering it mostly in a different direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.160
to the original light. So it is taking&nbsp;
out those frequencies of the light. And an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.840
emission spectrum is just made of that scattered&nbsp;
light. Light is made of particles called photons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.320
and the energy of a photon is based solely on its&nbsp;
frequency which you can see from this equation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.200
E=hf where h is a constant called Planck’s&nbsp;
constant. Also the frequency and wavelength are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.320
related to each other inversely by this equation,&nbsp;
where c is the speed of light in a vacuum.
&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.920
Here we have part of the emission spectrum for&nbsp;
hydrogen. You’ll notice that there are three groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:06.160
of spectral lines. There is a good reason for&nbsp;
this. The first group, called the Lymann group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.480
is made from all the decays from high energy&nbsp;
states down to the ground state. The other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:15.440
groups come from energy decays from above&nbsp;
down to higher energy levels in a similar way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.520
That’s why the lines are grouped like this. Note&nbsp;
that this is not to scale, in reality these lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:27.040
are far more spread out in the wavelength scale,&nbsp;
and there are more series at longer wavelengths.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.880
You should also note that I’ve been using&nbsp;
hydrogen as examples because it has only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.440
got one proton and one electron, so it&nbsp;
has got a relatively simple spectrum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.960
But the spectra of other elements are more complex&nbsp;
and a real observation of many many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.720
gasses overlapping is incredibly complex.
Now you know all the basics of atomic spectra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.520
but it is also worth knowing about fine structure&nbsp;
and hyperfine structure. These are where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.160
if you look at a spectral line really closely&nbsp;
you’ll see that they are often made of two or more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.680
spectral lines that are incredibly close together.&nbsp;
The atomic spectra we’ve talked about so far are an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.160
idealised model, but when you add in the spin of&nbsp;
the electron and also relativistic effects you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:11.120
the fine structure splitting of energy levels.&nbsp;
And beyond that the hyperfine structure is seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:16.160
as small shifts and splittings of these energy&nbsp;
levels caused by interaction between the electrons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:21.040
and the nucleus through their mutual magnetic&nbsp;
or electric fields as well as other effects.
&nbsp;

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.960
Now it is important to note that what I’ve&nbsp;
described so far isn’t the only way that light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.840
interacts with matter. There are a few other ways&nbsp;
that you should know about. The most common is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:35.520
scattering which happens when electrons are&nbsp;
free to wiggle about when light shines on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.080
them called Rayleigh scattering. You don’t need&nbsp;
the rules of quantum physics to describe this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.200
it is a classical effect of light being a&nbsp;
vibrating electromagnetic wave which interacts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.520
with the electric charge of the electrons&nbsp;
causing them to wobble and then become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.320
an oscillating source of more electromagnetic&nbsp;
waves which radiate out in all directions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.000
This scattering called Rayleigh scattering is&nbsp;
what causes the blue colour of the sky and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.800
red colour of sunsets. It is also why metals&nbsp;
are very reflective, because they have loads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:08.560
of free electrons that move around very easily.&nbsp;
We’ve also got Raman scattering when light shines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.920
on molecules, and can vibrate the molecules in&nbsp;
different modes and has its own spectroscopy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:19.040
called Raman spectroscopy. And there’s also&nbsp;
Brillouin scattering where light can lose or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:23.680
gain energy when interacting with vibrational&nbsp;
waves in the atomic lattices of solids.
&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.160
Finally let’s look at the applications of atomic&nbsp;
spectra. I’ve already mentioned the array of uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:37.040
in astrophysics. The other main use is to see&nbsp;
what elements and compounds something is made of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:41.120
This is used in chemistry labs to see what&nbsp;
a sample is made of. Or even using the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:46.560
hyperfine structure to delve into the nucleus with&nbsp;
nuclear magnetic resonance and other techniques.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.880
Lasers come from controlling the&nbsp;
emissions from specific energy levels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:56.240
We can also harness the accuracy and repeatability&nbsp;
of the energy levels. For example atomic clocks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:01.840
all need a standard oscillator to keep the same&nbsp;
time, so use the light from a specific hyperfine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.920
transition of the cesium-133 atom which is&nbsp;
also how the SI units of distance the metre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:13.200
and time the second are defined, and so this&nbsp;
also sets the definition of the speed of light.
&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.533
So that’s my introduction to&nbsp;
atomic spectra, very cool stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

